  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale: We leverage the provided parent solution as a foundation with several key improvements. The core idea is to maintain the beneficial structure (two humps) but enhance the optimization through adaptive dimensionality reduction via polynomial interpolation. This approach bridges the gap between continuous and discrete representations while affording gradient-based refinement. Unlike random searches, this method systematically explores promising regions and distinguishes between local correlation minima.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification: Our candidate functions represent step functions through piecewise constant approximations using polynomial interpolation at varying dimensions. The objective remains minimizing the maximum shift correlation:\n    min h C5 = max_k \u222b[0,2] h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n    Constraints:\n        $\\sum_{i=1}^{n} h_i \\cdot dx = 1$ and $0\\leq h_i \\leq 1$\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\n   - Initialize using available parent solution (n=200)\n   - Adaptive scaling: Perform 10 optimization iterations with dimensions [150, 250, 350, 400]\n   - Projected gradient descent with dimension-dynamic adaptation\n   - Best solution tracking and fallback mechanism\n\n4. Search Choices:\n   - Polynomial interpolation degree: degree-1 (linear) as it balances interpolation accuracy and computational efficiency\n   - Dimension sweep: logarithmic growth to avoid dimension curse\n   - Minimal perturbation amplitude: \u00b15% of current best\n\n5. Compute Budget & Robustness:\n   - Dominant operation: Correlation evaluation (85% time)\n   - Time budget: 1100 seconds\n   - Early termination: For excessively large derivatives (gradient magnitude > 0.5)\n   - Fallback: 2-point uniform solution (10,10) if projection fails\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract:\n   - Enforce: sum(h)*dx == 1 and 0<=h[i]<=1\n   - Re-evaluate final solution using np.correlate\n   - Output: (h_values, computed_C5, n_points)\n   - Fallback solution guaranteed to pass validation",
